1013 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (The Product Category under the Maker-to-Market Initiative is designed to move beyond competition-based prototyping and into real product development for real customers. Unlike the robotics challenge, which focuses on technical execution within a defined problem space, the Product Category places teams in a product-engineering and entrepreneurship pipeline where they are required to design, prototype, refine, and present solutions to real-world problems faced by Small and Medium Enterprises \(SMEs\).)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [1]
>>
endobj
1014 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (Participants will not be solving hypothetical challenges. Instead, each selected team will work on a clearly defined problem sourced directly from partner organizations and SMEs. The objective is to produce a functional, manufacturable, and presentable product prototype that demonstrates technical soundness, user relevance, and commercial viability. This category intentionally mirrors early-stage startup and product development environments, requiring teams to balance engineering decisions with usability, cost, manufacturability, and business considerations.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
1015 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 326 0 R
/E (The Product Category emphasizes long-term value creation: students gain exposure to mechanical fabrication, electronics and embedded systems, product iteration, documentation, pitching, and stakeholder engagement, while SMEs receive practical solutions tailored to their operational needs. The outcome is a learning experience grounded in industry relevance, accountability, and measurable impact.)
/Pg 48 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
